# Copy to .dev.vars (gitignored) to enable real GitHub App sign-in locally.
# GitHub App permissions: Contents (read-only) and Metadata (read-only).
# Keep webhooks, Device Flow, and "Request user authorization during
# installation" disabled for the current explicit /api/github/login flow.
# Register this callback URL in the GitHub App:
#   http://localhost:5173/api/auth/callback/github
ENVIRONMENT="development"
DEV_AUTH="enabled"
# Owner-only setup replay/reset controls; safe with real GitHub OAuth because
# the server also requires PLATFORM_OWNER_LOGINS membership.
ONBOARDING_DEV_TOOLS="enabled"
EXECUTION_PROVIDER="fixture"
# 32+ characters. Signs sessions and the setup page's check-off commands,
# so a portal without it cannot offer those commands. Any long random
# string works locally; generate a real one per deployment.
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET="local-development-only-not-a-real-secret-0000"
BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:5173"
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=""
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=""
GITHUB_APP_SLUG=""
# Leave empty while the GitHub App webhook is disabled.
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
# Optional owner/repo allowlist for the course template.
GITHUB_TEMPLATE_REPO=""
GITHUB_TEMPLATE_REPO_ID=""
# Platform owners. GitHub logins, comma-separated, matched case-insensitively
# (auth/roles.ts). This is the only role that lives in configuration, because
# it is the root of trust: owners manage the staff roster, so an owner list
# that could be edited from inside the app would let anyone who reached the
# roster make themselves an owner. It also means a fresh database is never
# locked out, since owners are staff automatically and can add the first
# roster row. Changing it needs a redeploy, which is the right cost for a
# list that changes about once a year.
PLATFORM_OWNER_LOGINS="your-github-login"

# There is no PLATFORM_STAFF_LOGINS. The staff roster moved into the
# platform_staff table in migration 0031 and is edited by an owner under
# PLATFORM STAFF in the admin console, so a cohort change no longer needs a
# secret edit and a redeploy. Being a TA for a specific team is still separate
# again: that is a team_tas row, assigned per team in the same console.

# Discord service-binding policy and Modal runner callback signing.
COURSE_GUILD_ID=""
# Required only for live team run bubbles. Keep this in .dev.vars locally and
# set it with `wrangler secret put DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN` in deployed environments.
DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN=""
PUBLIC_ORIGIN="http://localhost:5173"
RUNNER_SIGNING_KEY_ID="runner-v1"
# SECRET. Leave empty unless you are running against real Modal locally; empty
# is read as "not configured" (worker/env.ts sets emptyStringAsUndefined), which
# is the correct state for fixture mode. To generate, set, or rotate the real
# value across Modal and both Cloudflare environments, follow
# docs/runbooks/rotate-signing-secret.md. The notes below explain what it does.
RUNNER_SIGNING_SECRET=""
MODAL_RUNNER_URL=""
RUN_STALE_AFTER_SECONDS="3600"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Running against the real Modal runner, locally
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Everything above this line is the fixture setup, and it is the right default:
# fixture mode advances runs from a wall clock and never sends anything
# anywhere, so it costs nothing and needs no credentials. Turning it off means
# every practice run you start spends real sandbox time.
#
# Change these values in your own .dev.vars only. apps/portal/wrangler.jsonc
# carries its own copy for the deployed staging and production workers, and
# editing it there is a separate decision that costs money.
#
# Before starting anything, run the offline check. It reports what is missing
# and why, and it compares the two HMAC implementations by computing signatures
# with both rather than by reading them:
#
#   python apps/runner-modal/tools/preflight_dispatch.py
#
# Then docs/runbooks/gate-1-modal.md, which is the ordered procedure.
#
# EXECUTION_PROVIDER
#   "fixture" or "modal". With "fixture", dispatch() in
#   worker/services/run-actions.ts returns immediately and no job is ever sent.
#   With "modal", starting a run posts one signed job to MODAL_RUNNER_URL.
#   The value is written onto each run row, so a run started under fixture
#   keeps behaving as a fixture run after you switch back.
#
# MODAL_RUNNER_URL
#   Not a secret. The https endpoint `modal deploy` prints for submit_job, of
#   the form https://<workspace>--cogworks-runner-submit-job.modal.run. Find it
#   again with `modal app list`. Leave empty to keep dispatch unconfigured.
#
# RUNNER_SIGNING_SECRET
#   SECRET. Never commit it, never paste it into a runbook or a chat, never
#   print it. It must be byte-identical to the value inside the Modal secret
#   named cogworks-runner-signing (`modal secret list` shows the name; the
#   value is only in Modal and wherever it was first generated). It signs both
#   directions: the Worker signs jobs with it, and Modal signs the callbacks
#   the Worker verifies. A mismatch is a bare 401 with no diagnostics on either
#   side, which is why tools/preflight_dispatch.py, tools/verify_dispatch.py,
#   and tools/probe_callback.py all exist. In a deployed environment set it with
#   `wrangler secret put RUNNER_SIGNING_SECRET`, never as a plain var, and once
#   per environment: Wrangler secrets do not copy from staging to production.
#
#   To supply it for one command without writing it down:
#     RUNNER_SIGNING_SECRET=... pnpm dev
#
#   Generating, setting, or changing it is one ordered procedure across three
#   systems, and doing it out of order strands runs for an hour. It is written
#   down in docs/runbooks/rotate-signing-secret.md; do not improvise it.
#
# RUNNER_SIGNING_KEY_ID
#   Not a secret. Both sides compare it before checking the signature, so it
#   must match Modal's RUNNER_SIGNING_KEY_ID exactly. "runner-v1" unless the
#   secret has been rotated. Incrementing it buys no overlap, because only one
#   key is ever active; what it buys is a distinguishable 401 ("Unknown runner
#   signing key" rather than "Runner signature is invalid") so a straggler
#   signed under the old key is not mistaken for a mistyped secret.
#
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN
#   Not a secret, and the value most likely to stop a first real run. Two
#   independent requirements. It must start with https:// or
#   assertModalConfigured throws 501 before anything is sent. And Modal posts
#   every run event to <PUBLIC_ORIGIN>/api/internal/v1/runner/events from its
#   own network, so an origin that only resolves on your laptop dispatches
#   fine and then strands the run in `queued` until the stale reaper fails it
#   an hour later. https://localhost:5173 satisfies the first and fails the
#   second. Use a public tunnel to your dev server, or accept that a local
#   dispatch is fire-and-forget and read the result from Modal's own logs.
#
# RUNNER_IMAGE_DIGEST
#   Not a secret, and optional: runner.ts falls back to a constant when it is
#   unset. It selects nothing. Modal picks a sandbox image by name, and this
#   value's only use is as one of three inputs to the environmentDigest
#   reported on a completed run. So a placeholder cannot fail a dispatch; what
#   it does is make every run's reproducibility record a hash of the same word,
#   so two runs on genuinely different images are recorded as identical. Set it
#   from the id `python apps/runner-modal/tools/deploy.py` prints, which looks
#   like `published cogworks-runner-week3 -> im-...`. Writing it as
#   <name>@<id> keeps both facts in the record.
#
# RUNNER_PYTHON_VERSION
#   Not a secret, and it does not apply to every benchmark. Weeks 1 and 3
#   always run student code under a pinned CPython 3.8.20 venv baked into their
#   images, so buildRunJob ignores this for them. It is the default for the
#   rest. Leave it unset unless you know why you are changing it.
#
# EXECUTION_PROVIDER="modal"
# MODAL_RUNNER_URL="https://<workspace>--cogworks-runner-submit-job.modal.run"
# RUNNER_SIGNING_SECRET=""
# RUNNER_SIGNING_KEY_ID="runner-v1"
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN="https://<your-public-tunnel-host>"
# RUNNER_IMAGE_DIGEST="cogworks-runner-week3@im-..."
